--TEST--
a value that does not fit a shape has the absent key named for it rather than the two shapes printed for it to diff, one key or several, and a parenthesised group reports the same mistake once — `rule:types/shape-type`, `rule:types/unions-and-mixed`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:types/shape-type` checks a shape structurally, so a source that does
// not fit it has failed on one named key rather than on being the wrong kind
// of thing. The mismatch prints both shapes; the help line says which key.
({age: int, name: string}) $one = {name: "x"};

// Two absent keys are named together, in the target shape's own order.
({x: int, y: int, z: int}) $two = {y: 2};

// A field present at the wrong type has nothing absent, so nothing is named
// and the two printed shapes are the whole diagnosis.
({x: int}) $typed = {x: "s"};

// The parentheses add nothing to the mistake, so they add no second report.
({age: int, name: string}) $group = ({name: "x"});
echo "unreachable\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0401]: expected `{age: int, name: string}`, found `{name: string}`
  --> case.nvs:5:35
%A
  = help: `age` is required here, and this value does not supply it
%A
error[E0401]: expected `{x: int, y: int, z: int}`, found `{y: int}`
  --> case.nvs:8:35
%A
  = help: these keys are required here, and this value supplies none of them: `x`, `z`
%A
error[E0401]: expected `{x: int}`, found `{x: string}`
   --> case.nvs:12:21
%A
error[E0401]: expected `{age: int, name: string}`, found `{name: string}`
   --> case.nvs:15:38
%A
   = help: `age` is required here, and this value does not supply it
%A
